#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055548} :activity/id #uuid "0b4acd9e-f257-5db1-a9ff-f4bc70fb528a" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049844 :topic/id #uuid "181a217b-aefb-5b16-abf7-81644bd30ca2" :topic/math-academy-id 96} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055548 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186056066 :tutorial/content "Let's take a number, say, $-3.$ We can depict it on a number line as follows:\n\n![](images/de/ded8743284f207bff35eba24617fd4245a69d439ed151d09eed564813f9d72fb.png)\n\nThe *distance* between the number $-3$ and $0$ is known as the **absolute value** of $-3.$ We write it as\n\n$$\n|-3| = {\\color{red}3}.\n$$\n\nSimilarly, $|\\,2\\,|$, the absolute value of the number $2$, is the distance between $2$ and $0$:\n\n![](images/f4/f44fcd05fb0932083d48c8b11892247d8de8fbc46ccf7186fb12843702181aea.png)\n\nWe write $|\\,2\\,| = {\\color{red}2}.$\n\nSo, the absolute value simply \n\n- *removes the minus sign*, if the number is negative, or\n\n- does nothing, if the number is non-negative.\n\nSometimes, we can be asked to evaluate an expression involving the absolute value function, like $|x +1| + 5$ when $x=-3.$ In this case, we evaluate the expression inside the absolute value before simplifying the rest of the expression: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|x+1|+5 & = \\\\ |(-3)+1|+5 & = \\\\ |-2|+5 & = \\\\ 2+5 & = \\\\ 7 & \\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "3325f9a7-d389-562e-ac1b-4eb26671f84b" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8817 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "2ecbe2d3-3bf6-5c94-9e47-13b35a557d38" :step/math-academy-id 147765 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055549}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055549 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054626 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054470 :question/id #uuid "af2c36f6-7258-56b3-aabc-c0736bdfb6a5" :question/math-academy-id "e-14209"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2a5017c8-1933-515c-980a-c4b6a8a46334" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048599} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049688} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050127}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055102} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055113} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055175} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481347} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481392} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481436}] :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating an Expression With One Absolute Value"} :step/id #uuid "4957b9ec-7c2c-553a-b341-f367fd1d5e59" :step/math-academy-id 147766 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055550}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055550 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054627 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054471 :question/id #uuid "2af15364-dc4d-52c1-a519-02e8568279ae" :question/math-academy-id "e-14212"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9d29b448-4fd6-51bc-b2f0-92e3d909f299" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055103} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055104} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055114} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481358} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481394} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481409}] :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating an Expression With Two Absolute Values"} :step/id #uuid "ea43903e-13f4-5326-a1ae-670194c48ad9" :step/math-academy-id 147770}] :activity/title "Absolute Value Expressions" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186054936}]}
